{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528111514,"sessionID":"ses_e2dcb0d53ffeBaw5RhZ4h4KChT","part":{"partID":"prt_1d235eb88001jYgw9GnQtOUUip","sessionID":"ses_e2dcb0d53ffeBaw5RhZ4h4KChT","messageID":"msg_1d235d8f4001BPkHMaMYFcs00B","type":"tool","id":"call_0e316a4a00954dd886ac0a8e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"INSTALL-SKILLED.md","offset":40,"limit":40},"output":"Read file INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 40-79\n40: Build the Spec Kit first. The runtimes' session hooks load this build.\n41: \n42: ```bash\n43: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit install\n44: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit run build\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Then build the Skill Advisor. Its build uses the Spec Kit's TypeScript compiler, so it comes second. The last command checks the advisor's CLI. `list-tools` answers offline, with no daemon running.\n48: \n49: ```bash\n50: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n51: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n52: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json\n53: ```\n54: \n55: ### Check the Install\n56: \n57: Open the repository in any of the six runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin or Pi. Then run:\n58: \n59: ```\n60: /doctor:setup\n61: ```\n62: \n63: It checks the install without changing anything. It names one repair command for each failure.\n64: \n65: Codex, Cursor and Pi spell commands with a hyphen, as in `/doctor-setup`. Devin reaches the spec-kit workflows through its `/system-spec-kit` skill.\n66: \n67: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved.\n68: \n69: ### Add Code Mode\n70: \n71: Code Mode is the bridge your runtime uses to call external MCP tools. It builds separately from the two builds above. Run:\n72: \n73: ```\n74: /doctor:mcp install\n75: ```\n76: \n77: It installs the dependencies and builds Code Mode when its `dist` is missing or stale. It configures the `.utcp_config.json` manuals. It registers Code Mode in the seven project config files, two of them for Claude. Each write waits for its own approval. Add `--runtime <name>` to register one runtime only: `opencode`, `claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `pi` or `devin`.\n78: \n79: To check Code Mode later, run `/doctor:mcp debug`. It reports PASS, WARN or FAIL per check, and `--fix` adds guided repair. Provider keys live in `.env`, which only Code Mode reads. Section 1 of [`.env.example`](.env.example) lists them. An agent cannot read that file: the [dotenv guard](.skilled/hooks/env-secret-guard/README.md) refuses it on every runtime and offers key names or a fingerprint instead. The [Code Mode install guide](.skilled/skills/mcp-code-mode/INSTALL-GUIDE.md) covers the manual steps.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 80]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file INSTALL-SKILLED.md, lines 40-79\n40: Build the Spec Kit first. The runtimes' session hooks load this build.\n41: \n42: ```bash\n43: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit install\n44: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit run build\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Then build the Skill Advisor. Its build uses the Spec Kit's TypeScript compiler, so it comes second. The last command checks the advisor's CLI. `list-tools` answers offline, with no daemon running.\n48: \n49: ```bash\n50: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n51: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n52: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json\n53: ```\n54: \n55: ### Check the Install\n56: \n57: Open the repository in any of the six runtimes: OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin or Pi. Then run:\n58: \n59: ```\n60: /doctor:setup\n61: ```\n62: \n63: It checks the install without changing anything. It names one repair command for each failure.\n64: \n65: Codex, Cursor and Pi spell commands with a hyphen, as in `/doctor-setup`. Devin reaches the spec-kit workflows through its `/system-spec-kit` skill.\n66: \n67: Codex runs a project hook only after you approve it, so open `/hooks` in a Codex session at the repository root and approve each entry it lists. Codex keeps each approval as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again, and `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` prints `PASS codex-hooks-approved` once the advisor's entry is approved.\n68: \n69: ### Add Code Mode\n70: \n71: Code Mode is the bridge your runtime uses to call external MCP tools. It builds separately from the two builds above. Run:\n72: \n73: ```\n74: /doctor:mcp install\n75: ```\n76: \n77: It installs the dependencies and builds Code Mode when its `dist` is missing or stale. It configures the `.utcp_config.json` manuals. It registers Code Mode in the seven project config files, two of them for Claude. Each write waits for its own approval. Add `--runtime <name>` to register one runtime only: `opencode`, `claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `pi` or `devin`.\n78: \n79: To check Code Mode later, run `/doctor:mcp debug`. It reports PASS, WARN or FAIL per check, and `--fix` adds guided repair. Provider keys live in `.env`, which only Code Mode reads. Section 1 of [`.env.example`](.env.example) lists them. An agent cannot read that file: the [dotenv guard](.skilled/hooks/env-secret-guard/README.md) refuses it on every runtime and offers key names or a fingerprint instead. The [Code Mode install guide](.skilled/skills/mcp-code-mode/INSTALL-GUIDE.md) covers the manual steps.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 80]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"INSTALL-SKILLED.md\",\"offset\":40,\"limit\":40}"},"time":{"start":1794528111496,"end":1794528111514}}}}
